WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.385
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.685 --> 00:00:08.758
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:17.933
Unit twenty-three: Democracy and Participation. In this unit, you will discuss how citizens take part in public life.

4
00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:20.429
civic engagement.

5
00:00:21.129 --> 00:00:26.251
civic engagement means citizens taking part in community and public life.

6
00:00:26.551 --> 00:00:29.468
Volunteering is one form of civic engagement.

7
00:00:30.168 --> 00:00:33.749
Your turn. Say: civic engagement.

8
00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:37.926
civic engagement.

9
00:00:38.917 --> 00:00:40.231
voter turnout.

10
00:00:40.931 --> 00:00:44.684
voter turnout means the percentage of eligible people who vote.

11
00:00:44.984 --> 00:00:48.272
Voter turnout is often lower among young people.

12
00:00:48.972 --> 00:00:51.845
Your turn. Say: voter turnout.

13
00:00:54.545 --> 00:00:55.639
voter turnout.

14
00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:57.779
polarisation.

15
00:00:58.479 --> 00:01:02.847
polarisation means the division of society into opposing groups.

16
00:01:03.147 --> 00:01:06.261
Social media can increase polarisation.

17
00:01:06.961 --> 00:01:09.903
Your turn. Say: polarisation.

18
00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:13.697
polarisation.

19
00:01:14.683 --> 00:01:16.079
grassroots campaign.

20
00:01:16.779 --> 00:01:21.902
grassroots campaign means a movement started by ordinary people rather than leaders.

21
00:01:22.202 --> 00:01:24.956
A grassroots campaign saved the local park.

22
00:01:25.656 --> 00:01:28.750
Your turn. Say: grassroots campaign.

23
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:32.996
grassroots campaign.

24
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:35.251
deliberation.

25
00:01:35.951 --> 00:01:39.716
deliberation means careful discussion before making a decision.

26
00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:42.770
Good decisions require time for deliberation.

27
00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.540
Your turn. Say: deliberation.

28
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.381
deliberation.

29
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:52.802
civil society.

30
00:01:53.502 --> 00:01:59.043
civil society means organisations and groups outside government and business.

31
00:01:59.343 --> 00:02:03.014
Civil society groups support education and health.

32
00:02:03.714 --> 00:02:06.738
Your turn. Say: civil society.

33
00:02:09.438 --> 00:02:10.939
civil society.

34
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.357
political apathy.

35
00:02:14.057 --> 00:02:17.566
political apathy means lack of interest in politics.

36
00:02:17.866 --> 00:02:21.456
Political apathy is common when people feel unheard.

37
00:02:22.156 --> 00:02:25.749
Your turn. Say: political apathy.

38
00:02:28.449 --> 00:02:29.857
political apathy.

39
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.490
representation gap.

40
00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:38.533
representation gap means the difference between a population and those who represent it.

41
00:02:38.833 --> 00:02:42.713
There is a representation gap for young people in decision-making.

42
00:02:43.413 --> 00:02:47.610
Your turn. Say: representation gap.

43
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.973
representation gap.

44
00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:55.122
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:55.422 --> 00:03:00.173
Ms. Sarah, why are young people around the world often uninterested in politics?

46
00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:06.118
Political apathy usually grows when people feel their voices don't matter.

47
00:03:07.417 --> 00:03:11.622
Is that why voter turnout is low among students in many countries?

48
00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:17.659
Often, yes. Were young people better represented, they might participate more.

49
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.704
There is definitely a representation gap.

50
00:03:23.017 --> 00:03:28.641
Civic engagement isn't only voting, though. Grassroots campaigns can achieve a great deal.

51
00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.389
Like the students who organised a clean-up of the riverside?

52
00:03:34.683 --> 00:03:39.124
Exactly. Had they waited for someone else, nothing would have changed.

53
00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:43.751
Grammar focus: Inverted conditionals in argument.

54
00:03:44.051 --> 00:03:47.235
Inverted conditionals sound formal and persuasive.

55
00:03:47.535 --> 00:03:54.019
Were young people better represented, they would participate more. Had they waited, nothing would have changed.

56
00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:57.711
Should turnout fall further, reforms will be needed.

57
00:03:58.011 --> 00:04:03.819
Inverted conditionals make arguments sound formal and persuasive in essays and debates.

58
00:04:04.817 --> 00:04:07.573
Now read and listen. More Than a Vote.

59
00:04:07.873 --> 00:04:13.855
Democracy depends not only on elections but also on active citizens.

60
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:21.201
Around the world, voter turnout among young people tends to be lower than among older generations.

61
00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:28.197
Researchers link this to political apathy and a sense that institutions are distant.

62
00:04:29.483 --> 00:04:34.153
However, young people are often highly active in other forms of civic engagement.

63
00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:42.221
They organise grassroots campaigns, volunteer for civil society groups and debate issues online.

64
00:04:43.517 --> 00:04:49.080
Online debate, though, can also increase polarisation when people only hear similar views.

65
00:04:50.383 --> 00:04:57.433
Deliberation, in which citizens listen carefully to opposing arguments, is essential for healthy societies.

66
00:04:58.717 --> 00:05:03.712
Were more spaces created for respectful discussion, participation might grow.

67
00:05:05.017 --> 00:05:07.855
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:08.155 --> 00:05:10.804
Lack of interest in politics is blank.

69
00:05:11.104 --> 00:05:17.277
A, civic engagement. B, political apathy. C, deliberation.

70
00:05:21.283 --> 00:05:24.003
The answer is B: political apathy.

71
00:05:25.517 --> 00:05:27.806
blank I known, I would have helped.

72
00:05:28.106 --> 00:05:31.075
A, If. B, Had. C, Were.

73
00:05:35.083 --> 00:05:37.153
The answer is B: Had.

74
00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:41.044
blank you need assistance, please call us.

75
00:05:41.344 --> 00:05:44.464
A, Should. B, Had. C, Were.

76
00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.216
The answer is A: Should.

77
00:05:52.717 --> 00:05:55.889
The division of society into opposing groups is blank.

78
00:05:56.189 --> 00:06:02.246
A, polarisation. B, voter turnout. C, civil society.

79
00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.946
The answer is A: polarisation.

80
00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:14.110
According to the text, what can increase polarisation?

81
00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:20.571
A, volunteering. B, online debate with similar views. C, deliberation.

82
00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:28.115
The answer is B: online debate with similar views.

83
00:06:29.617 --> 00:06:35.439
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can young people participate more in their communities?

84
00:06:40.739 --> 00:06:45.873
Part two: Use an inverted conditional, give two methods and one example.

85
00:06:52.173 --> 00:07:08.980
Were schools to teach civic skills, students would feel more confident to participate. Volunteering and student councils are two effective methods. My university's environmental club, for example, persuaded the city to install recycling bins.

86
00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.907
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:12.917 --> 00:07:18.321
Great work today! You learned eight new words and inverted conditionals in argument.

88
00:07:18.621 --> 00:07:22.213
Today's value is community. Help the people in your town.

89
00:07:22.513 --> 00:07:25.804
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:26.104 --> 00:07:28.675
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

